    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.title": "25 Years of Landsat for All 399 Municipalities of Paraná",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.subtitle": "A vertical Landsat timelapse (2000-2025) for every municipality in Paraná, plus a change ranking that shows where the landscape actually moved.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.category": "Remote Sensing / Data Visualization",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.location": "Paraná, Brazil",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.scale": "399 videos",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.outcome": "399 of 399 videos generated and validated (14.2 s and 354 frames each, 5.7 GB in total), and a ranking of every municipality by visible change: Ortigueira leads in absolute area (170 km²) and Miraselva in share of territory (13.5%).",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.card_desc": "A 1080x1920 Landsat timelapse (2000-2025) for each of Paraná's 399 municipalities, with sensor gaps, stripes and flicker handled by explicit rules, plus a ranking of visible landscape change.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.card_benefit": "399 of 399 videos validated and every municipality ranked by how much its landscape changed in 25 years.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.overview1": "Google Earth Timelapse shows the planet; this project does the same at the scale people care about, their own municipality. For each of Paraná's 399 municipalities the pipeline builds 26 annual cloud-free Landsat composites (2000-2025) and renders a 1080x1920 video for phones, ready for social media and local news.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.overview2": "Making 26 years of Landsat look continuous is mostly about defects: Landsat 7 stripes after 2003, the 2012 gap between Landsat 5 and 8, detector striping, haze and seasonal flicker. Each one is handled by an explicit, documented and tested rule.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.overview3": "The same frames feed a change analysis: the 2000-2002 median against 2023-2025, pixel by pixel, inside each municipal boundary, ignoring crop rotation and pre-existing water. Without the water mask, the colour change of the Itaipu reservoir put Foz do Iguaçu first at 20%; the real figure is 0.8%.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.method1": "IBGE municipal mesh to a 9:16 bounding box per municipality with a 10% margin (EPSG:3857).",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.method2": "Dry-season (15 April to 15 October) median of Landsat Collection 2 Level 2 scenes under 50% cloud, masked with QA_PIXEL and QA_RADSAT.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.method3": "Sensor schedule: Landsat 5 until 2011, Landsat 7 only until 2002, Landsat 8 and 9 from 2013; 2012 interpolated from 2011 and 2013.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.method4": "Stripe removal, gaps filled from the nearest valid year and brightness harmonised to the series median to remove flicker.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.method5": "Resumable batch rendering at 25 fps in H.264, every file validated with ffprobe.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.method6": "Change ranking: stable colour change above a threshold, a 3x3 noise filter, and water handled with JRC Global Surface Water.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.limits1": "2000-2012 frames are sparser than 2013-2025: late Landsat 5 had few passes, so some patches come from neighbouring years.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.limits2": "2012 is interpolated, not observed.",
    "cs.landsat-timelapse-parana.limits3": "The change classes are a brightness proxy on RGB frames, not a land-cover classification.",
